  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nCloudiness in the wake of the upper level low will continue to\nerode, leaving clear skies today. With light east winds,\ntemperatures will be slow to recover, with highs only in the mid\nto upper 60s. Some of the model suites keep temperatures only \nin the lower 60s due to shallower mixing. But with full sun, the\nwarmer solutions are favored despite all of the recent rain. \nWith light winds tonight, expect lows in the 40s at most \nlocations, with some readings near 40 in the usual cooler\nnocturnal spots of far western Kansas.\n\nDespite an upper level ridge over the Rockies, surface high\npressure will tend to ridge back to the southwest into Kansas\nthrough the weekend and into Monday, preventing any downslope \nflow. But temperatures will be seasonal and pleasant, especially\ngiven the relatively light winds, with highs in the mid to \nupper 70s. Some 80 degree readings are possible in far western \nKansas by Monday.\n\nWarmer weather will arrive by Tuesday as the mid level flow\nbecomes more westerly and weak downslope flow develops on the\nhigh plains. Highs in the mid 80s to even upper 80s can be \nexpected Wednesday through Friday, with clear to mostly clear \nskies each day. These temperatures are about 10 degrees above \nclimatology. The breeziest days should be Monday and Tuesday as \nthe warm air begins to advect into Kansas from the south and \nsouthwest. But light winds are favored by mid to late next \nweek. The various ensemble means indicate near 0% chances for\nmeasurable rain for all of next week.",
